# The two tools the license inventory is generated and enforced with, pinned.
#
# Same rule as packaging/host-tools: what a release contains is decided in a
# reviewed file, not by whatever a package manager or a registry resolves on the
# day. `cargo install` would build whatever the crate's own lockfile allows and
# take minutes doing it; these are the upstream release binaries, checked
# against the digest this procedure was verified with.
#
# Both are pinned at an exact version rather than a rolling tag, so a new
# upstream release cannot change what the staleness check in CI compares
# against. Moving to a newer one is a deliberate change here, followed by
# regenerating the inventory and committing whatever actually changed.
#
# **x86_64 only, deliberately.** The inventory does not depend on the machine
# that generates it: about.toml declares both release architectures, so the
# dependency graph is the union of what either artifact contains. Checking it
# once is checking it for both, and pinning an aarch64 binary nothing runs
# would be configuration with no build behind it.

# cargo-about, which reads Cargo.lock and each crate's own license file.
# 0.7.1, digest confirmed against upstream's published .sha256 (2026-09-20).
EPHERVEX_CARGO_ABOUT_VERSION=0.7.1
EPHERVEX_CARGO_ABOUT_URL=https://github.com/EmbarkStudios/cargo-about/releases/download/0.7.1/cargo-about-0.7.1-x86_64-unknown-linux-musl.tar.gz
EPHERVEX_CARGO_ABOUT_SHA256=395cde37f70a77f2bdb0eeaded96e294b0338482b937b0e813f61d1221f06642

